On 2008-06-28, Carl <carlhnelson@gmail.com> wrote:
> I was just playing (what I thought was) a very careful orcish rogue.
> ...
> This death was preventable since I should have expected a wand wielder
> in the room having not found the level's wand yet.  However, do people
> who pull off ascension streaks really play this carefully?

Having not found the level's wand yet, you should certainly expect it to
be in the room, and of course, no idea what it will be.  That much I'd
expect of any serial ascender.  To predict that it would be lightning is
another thing entirely, and it depends on whether you pause and think
about whether there are any ramifications of the piece of information
you just noticed -- ie. that the wand on this level is missing and thus
must be in the room.

If you DO remember to pause, I think it's well within reason that you 
would ask yourself "What kind of wands do monsters pick up", and then 
when opening the room door check to see what's on the floor -- and only
then decide whether or not you should be worried about a possible
offensive wand in the hands of a critter.  (Actually not opening the door
from fear is the type of maneuver I typically see of people who are too 
paranoid to consistently ascend more than once every two months.)

My longest streak is 6 games, and since I haven't played in some time, 
technically it's still going.  It's not one of the longest, but I
can say this: when I'm playing well, I remember to pause when I notice
things that are out of the ordinary and give a quick scan over what
might happen.  I usually notice that type of thing; it's whether I'm
focused enough on the game to remember to stop and review that decides
whether I live or die.

